From 86362b8590d0fd7a89bf3f90c77ead2c84807737 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Barattolo <barattolo@barattolo.store>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 17:53:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] timeout: give the maker a waiting window of his own, and put
 the order back in the book when he lets a take lapse

Upstream gives one window (`expiration_seconds`) to whoever holds the
waiting duty, and cancels the order outright when the party who lets it
lapse is its maker. That fits a taker, who has the phone in his hand; it
does not fit a maker, who published hours or days before and cannot know
when the order will be taken.

Two optional keys in `[mostro]`; with both absent the daemon behaves as
upstream:

- `maker_expiration_seconds`: the maker's own window when the duty is his.
  `find_order_by_seconds` selects with the shorter of the two windows and
  keeps the rows whose own window has elapsed; `reconfirm_timeout_eligibility`
  judges with the same.
- `maker_timeout_limit`: after how many maker lapses in a row the order is
  removed. Below it, a maker-responsible timeout republishes the order
  (same branch and same messages as a taker timeout). Lapses are counted
  in a side table kept out of the sqlx migrations, once per `taken_at`.
  Off on a node that bonds the maker.

Both are advertised in the info event, each only when in force.

The marketplace event for a republish that is the maker's fault is
`expired` + `maker-republished`: the server read every return to
`Pending` as a taker timeout.

`waiting-payment` is published as in-progress on sell orders too: a sell
order taken with the invoice already in the take stayed `pending` on the
book for the whole wait.

Re-anchored on v0.20.2 (29 Oct 2026): the info event's escrow tags are
built by `escrow_tags` (Lightning or Cashu mode, #1045), and the two
tags of this patch follow `bond_policy_tags` as before.
